  <section class="prose" aria-labelledby="success">
    <h2 id="success">Where students go</h2>

    <p>Previous students have gone on to a position in a United States Navy fleet band, the Chautauqua summer instrumental program, and a touring position with Tim Zimmerman and the King&rsquo;s Brass. Others have continued to undergraduate and graduate study at the Eastman School of Music, Rice University, Carnegie Mellon University, and DePaul University. An earlier student received a merit-based music scholarship to Harvard University.</p>

    <h2>In lessons</h2>

    <p>We start from the sound you want and work backward to the physical action that produces it &mdash; most problems that feel technical turn out to be problems of concept first. I want you to understand why a musical choice works rather than to copy mine, so you leave each lesson able to diagnose your own playing and with a practice plan rather than a list of corrections.</p>

    <p>Students arrive wanting different things from their playing, and the preparation is different for each. An orchestral audition, graduate school, a military band, a teaching job, chamber music, a freelance career &mdash; a studio that only prepares you for one of them is preparing most of its students for something they will never do.</p>

    <h2>Beyond the universities</h2>

    <p>I teach privately, and I have taught through the Academy of Music at the University of Northwestern, the Greater Twin Cities Youth Symphonies, the Mannes Preparatory Division, Boston College High School, and at Chautauqua &mdash; grade school through adult, in lessons, sectionals, masterclasses, and chamber coachings.</p>
  </section>

